00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:38.090
So why don't you Vikrum, tell me
a little bit what is Firebase?

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.640
MARK MANDEL: So Firebase's
mission statement

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.020
is to help developers build
extraordinary experiences.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.660
So we, in a nutshell,
provide a service

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.560
that lets you spin up
your app, and get started

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.240
with the least amount of work.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.915
And we make it easy for
iOS, Android, and web.

00:04:57.915 --> 00:05:00.652
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
it's mostly for mobile?

00:05:00.652 --> 00:05:02.190
SARA ROBINSON: It's
web and mobile.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.740
So we've got three client
libraries, JavaScript client

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.800
for the web, and then iOS and
Android clients for mobile.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.120
And then it's also a REST API.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.579
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so
what can you do with it?

00:05:11.579 --> 00:05:13.120
SARA ROBINSON: So
essentially there's

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.036
three core features that
are part of Firebase.

00:05:15.036 --> 00:05:16.960
The first is the
real time database,

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:18.800
which is also my favorite.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.942
So it's the NoSQL JSON database.

00:05:20.942 --> 00:05:22.400
And every piece of
data in Firebase

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:23.946
is referenced by a URL.

00:05:23.946 --> 00:05:27.170
So it makes it really easy
to write data directly

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:27.929
from the client.

00:05:27.929 --> 00:05:29.970
And the really cool thing
is that all the updates

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:30.940
happen in real time.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.687
So any client that's connected
to a Firebase application

00:05:33.687 --> 00:05:35.770
will see changes to that
data right as it happens.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.228
They don't have to
refresh the page

00:05:37.228 --> 00:05:39.400
or pull to refresh on mobile.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.065
So there's a big
wow factor there.

00:05:41.065 --> 00:05:43.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's one of the things

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:43.860
that I really like.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.560
And the cool thing
for me is that you

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.940
don't need to do it with
one of the libraries.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.020
You can also use the REST API.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.690
So you can use it with
Go, which is always nice.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:57.555
VIKRUM NIJJAR: And then the
second product that we have

00:05:57.555 --> 00:05:59.530
is static hosting.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.370
So this is
essentially a website,

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.550
but it gives you a
rare domain, CDN,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.840
and has a cell at
the push of a button.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.759
So it's really magical in
how easy it is to get set up.

00:06:11.759 --> 00:06:14.050
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, just
one command, Firebase deploy.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:16.560
And then your app is live
on it's own Firebaseapp.com

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:17.860
URL, which is pretty cool.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:19.276
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's very nice.

00:06:19.276 --> 00:06:21.302
So you're [? gofmt'd ?]
with HTTPS directly.

00:06:21.302 --> 00:06:22.760
SARA ROBINSON:
Yeah, you don't have

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.040
to worry about
acquiring the cert.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.675
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really cool.

00:06:26.675 --> 00:06:27.800
VIKRUM NIJJAR: That
is really cool.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.508
SARA ROBINSON: And
then the third feature

00:06:29.508 --> 00:06:31.030
is authentication.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.210
So if you ever
implemented OAuth,

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.025
it's not a very fun procedure.

00:06:35.025 --> 00:06:37.910
But Firebase luckily takes
care of all that for you.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:40.930
So we support a number
of different providers

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:44.310
so you can authenticate users
with Facebook, Twitter, GitHub,

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:48.180
Google, or email password
login or authentication,

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.752
or anonymous authentication.

00:06:49.752 --> 00:06:51.960
And you just need to freeze
in one of the third party

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:52.460
providers.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:54.460
Just enter your
API key in secret

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.044
into your Favorites dashboard.

00:06:56.044 --> 00:06:58.210
And then you can start
authenticating users directly

00:06:58.210 --> 00:06:59.525
from the client.

00:06:59.525 --> 00:07:00.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:03.544
That actually makes writing
applications much easier.

00:07:03.544 --> 00:07:05.710
Because I know that when
I'm writing an application,

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.710
the worst part is
when you're like,

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:11.900
now I need to add authentication
because I want to be safe.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:16.315
It's always fun to auth
too dense-- yeah, no, I

00:07:16.315 --> 00:07:16.899
don't like it.

00:07:16.899 --> 00:07:18.315
SARA ROBINSON:
Yeah, with Firebase

00:07:18.315 --> 00:07:20.260
it's just one method--
Call in JavaScript auth

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:21.350
with OAuth pop up.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:24.810
And then you've got user
authentication in your app.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.790
VIKRUM NIJJAR: So
the theme along all

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.970
of those three main
offerings that we

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:36.387
have is to try to move people as
fast as possible for their app

00:07:36.387 --> 00:07:37.970
without having to
worry about servers.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.260
And then they start
getting wrapped around axle

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.030
with getting those set up, and
provision and scaling them.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.936
And we try to end
all of that for you.

00:07:44.936 --> 00:07:46.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:52.354
So for a little bit of
context, how old is Firebase?

00:07:52.354 --> 00:07:53.020
How many years--

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.436
VIKRUM NIJJAR:
What's its history?

00:07:54.436 --> 00:07:57.515
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
What's the history?

00:07:57.515 --> 00:07:59.050
VIKRUM NIJJAR: So
Google acquired

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:02.560
Firebase in October 2014.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:06.830
And Firebase got its start
during the summer of 2011

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.160
while James and Andrew,
the co-founders,

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:11.290
were going through Y Combinator.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:15.600
And it's a little known fact-- I
guess it is a large known fact.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.910
So the same batch
that we went through,

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.304
Meteor also went
through that batch.

00:08:21.304 --> 00:08:22.720
Parse also went
through the batch.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.410
And then we were
also in that batch.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:28.250
So there was three client, back
end of the service offerings

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.435
all through YC during
the summer of 2011.

00:08:31.435 --> 00:08:32.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.350
And how did the company evolve?

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:37.380
How did you start?

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:38.890
Why did you start building this?

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:41.159
And why do you have
those three features?

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.920
How do you decide
what's what to Firebase?

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.050
VIKRUM NIJJAR: So
they were actually

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.655
going through YC with a
completely different company.

00:08:48.655 --> 00:08:51.170
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:54.490
It was a company called
Involve, actually,

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.780
that was a chat service.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:01.110
So this is sort of
a business where

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.130
you can add chat to your
website without having

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:06.180
to worry about servers.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.900
And the common drumbeat that we
got from our paying customers

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.140
at that time was, well,
can we add game data

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:13.350
on top of this chat bus?

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.620
Can we add comments?

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.520
And so there was
this recurring theme

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:21.660
that people wanted to have real
time features without having

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.750
to architect it or build it.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.010
And it was around
that time that we

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:30.370
decided to sort of generalize
that offering into a real time

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:31.470
database.

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:37.270
So we started out with just
the database that summer.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:40.135
And focused just
on that part of it.

00:09:40.135 --> 00:09:44.390
The hosting and authentication
came a couple years later.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:47.086
But we just focused on
the database at that time.

00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:50.780
SARA ROBINSON: And one way that
Firebase grew its user base

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.410
really early on was
talking directly

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:54.576
to the developers that were
using it from the start.

00:09:54.576 --> 00:09:56.720
So I know even before
I joined, you guys

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.020
went to a ton of
hackathons and would

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:02.930
try to get feedback and see
how people were using API

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.252
and then iterate on
the product from there.

00:10:05.252 --> 00:10:10.350
VIKRUM NIJJAR: So we actually
wrote no lines of code

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:11.950
before we started
doing feedback.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:15.880
So Andrew had mocked
out documentation

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:19.140
for a hypothetical API
and told people, hey,

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:22.170
if you were to
write an application

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.490
with this set of APIs,
what would it look like?

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.940
So we got a lot of feedback
around the ergonomics

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:30.570
of the API before we even
wrote any code, just to flesh

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.510
out is this something that
can be readily consumed.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:38.300
And so we did hundreds of
hours of video interviews

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:42.700
with developers once the
API did get fleshed down,

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:44.900
once an SDK did exist.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:48.010
And that was one
of the core tenants

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:54.580
of how we kept the developers'
interest so tightly in focus.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:57.460
Because if-- you're
sort of in a vacuum.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:59.639
And you're hacking away,
and you think this is cool.

00:10:59.639 --> 00:11:01.180
It doesn't really
matter if it's cool

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:05.540
if it's not solving your
developers' problems.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:09.560
And we had a very
regimented approach

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.170
to doing developer
feedbacks, which

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.940
included actually videos
and reviewing the videos,

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:16.708
and not having to worry about
taking notes when we were doing

00:11:16.708 --> 00:11:17.541
developer feedbacks.

00:11:17.541 --> 00:11:19.020
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, I was
always really surprised

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:20.740
when we were almost ready
to launch a new feature,

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.330
we would go through a round
of developer feedbacks

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:23.954
and find that there
was a lot of things

00:11:23.954 --> 00:11:26.246
that we just didn't even
think about that were revealed

00:11:26.246 --> 00:11:27.495
through the feedback sessions.

00:11:27.495 --> 00:11:29.422
We were able to improve
before we launched.

00:11:29.422 --> 00:11:31.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah
that's interesting,

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:32.740
because I know that
a lot of companies

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:36.050
do that kind of product
management and market studies

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:39.400
and so on for
non-software stuff.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.425
Before you design
something that is hardware,

00:11:41.425 --> 00:11:43.550
you let people play with
it and see how they do it.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:49.842
But I have not heard about that
same technique for APIs, which

00:11:49.842 --> 00:11:51.050
is actually a very good idea.

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:51.530
Really like it.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:52.400
MARK MANDEL: Makes
a lot of sense.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:53.429
Makes a lot of sense.

00:11:53.429 --> 00:11:55.220
Sara, you're talking
about talking directly

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:56.512
with customers.

00:11:56.512 --> 00:11:58.470
What are some of the cool
things you saw people

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.016
building with
Firebase, or have seen

00:12:00.016 --> 00:12:01.265
people building with Firebase?

00:12:01.265 --> 00:12:03.675
What sort of cool
examples can we find?

00:12:03.675 --> 00:12:05.800
SARA ROBINSON: So one of
my favorites to talk about

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:06.940
is Warby Parker.

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:08.930
They're a glasses company.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:11.134
They let you order
your glasses online.

00:12:11.134 --> 00:12:13.800
And then they send you a couple,
you choose which ones you like.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:14.966
And then send back the rest.

00:12:14.966 --> 00:12:17.680
They have a physical
store in New York.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.290
And that store has
pneumatic tubes.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:21.416
So when you order your
glasses at the register,

00:12:21.416 --> 00:12:23.290
they're shot down to
you via pneumatic tubes.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:27.980
And the tubes' notifications are
actually powered by Firebase.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:29.690
MARK MANDEL: I have
Warby Parker glasses.

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:32.280
VIKRUM NIJAR: Those glasses
are powered by Firebase.

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:32.470
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:34.170
MARK MANDEL: I knew there
was a reason I loved them.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.170
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:37.419
MARK MANDEL: I bought them
in the San Francisco store.

00:12:37.419 --> 00:12:39.040
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:40.452
I feel bad.

00:12:40.452 --> 00:12:42.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is very, very cool.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:42.300
What about you?

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:42.966
Do you have one?

00:12:42.966 --> 00:12:45.810
VIKRUM NIJAR: Yeah so
my favorite Firebase

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.280
app is called Roll20.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:51.940
So this is a tabletop
gaming app that

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:55.620
Riley the founder has
been with us, essentially

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:57.840
since the beginning.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:00.860
And I love gaming.

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:04.810
It's a platform built
on Firebase for gaming.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:08.360
It connects people
with one another

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:13.090
to not only build
games for themselves,

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:16.060
but also play with others.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:21.830
And it's one of the use cases
where with a very small team,

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.030
he was able to
leverage our technology

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:27.160
to build his product.

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:28.630
So yeah, roll20.net.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.600
VIKRUM NIJJAR: I was
looking at that right now.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:31.340
Looks very cool.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:32.590
Looks like a very cool game.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.592
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:13:34.592 --> 00:13:38.270
MARK MANDEL: And there's a case
study on the Firebase site.

00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:41.525
Yeah, I've read that before,
and it's really cool.

00:13:41.525 --> 00:13:43.525
Stuff like this, especially
in the gaming space,

00:13:43.525 --> 00:13:45.066
I especially like
because of the fact

00:13:45.066 --> 00:13:48.060
that you've got the real
time updates across web, iOS,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.350
Android, so you have that
cross-platform approach which

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:53.100
is really, really powerful.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.560
I think that the thing that we
as developers and engineers,

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:01.920
we sort of lose sight of how
ubiquitous technologies like

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:07.010
real time, there's a lot
hidden behind the details.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.520
Just because we see it in
Google Docs and Spreadsheets all

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:12.320
the time, or we
see it in Hangouts

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:15.900
doesn't mean it's very
accessible to every developer.

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:19.600
We have huge teams of
people that are building

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.460
out Hangouts and Google Docs.

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:26.360
And so by having this
feature set in Firebase,

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:29.870
we give developers the
ability to build those sorts

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:31.160
of features in their products.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:32.340
SARA ROBINSON:
Makes that real time

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:34.210
accessible to maybe
just a single developer

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:35.350
or a small startup.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:38.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that's
actually a very, very, very

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:41.250
interesting point-- the
fact that it looks easy,

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:43.192
but it is not.

00:14:43.192 --> 00:14:45.100
So how is it done?

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:48.180
You can you talk a little
bit about the secret sauce?

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.805
What's going on in
the Firebase servers?

00:14:50.805 --> 00:14:57.070
VIKRUM NIJJAR: Yeah, so
at a very high level,

00:14:57.070 --> 00:15:02.250
we fan out this JSON
as Sara mentioned,

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:05.280
to developer SDKs
that are plugged

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:06.440
into their applications.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:09.200
So at a very high level, that's
sort of what we're doing.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:12.970
Nuts and bolts of it are whether
it's a browser that's connected

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:17.770
or an iOS device we have, in
the wide majority of cases

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:20.920
a persistent socket that's
connected to our servers.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:25.360
And then we handle the
fanout of JSON between all

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:26.843
of those connected sockets.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:33.280
So the core Firebase
server is written in Scala.

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:37.210
We use the awesome Netty
networking pipeline

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:42.540
to handle WebSockets, to handle
REST, like Sarah mentioned.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.930
We have a long
polling implementation

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:50.230
where for browsers that
don't support websockets,

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:53.230
we fall back to.

00:15:53.230 --> 00:15:56.470
It's all custom
implemented Scala that's

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:57.750
backed by disk elsewhere.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:02.384
But for most part, all the magic
happens in our JVM process.

00:16:02.384 --> 00:16:03.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I think that that

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:06.790
may be the first time I hear
about something bringing

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:10.090
Scala at Google.

00:16:10.090 --> 00:16:12.060
I don't know that anyone
else that does Scala.

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:17.277
So you have Scala on the JVM,
and you're an SRE for that.

00:16:17.277 --> 00:16:18.610
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that's right.

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:19.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's pretty unique.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.870
MARK MANDEL: Interesting,
very interesting.

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:23.130
Well, that's very cool.

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:25.760
So we were talking a
little bit previously

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:27.556
about gaming in Firebase.

00:16:27.556 --> 00:16:28.680
I got particularly excited.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:32.160
I saw there was a recent
Unity, Unity 3D plug-in.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:35.450
So if people aren't aware, Unity
is an engine for doing games

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:37.230
in both 3D and 2D.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:38.990
So there's a Unity
plugin for Firebase

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:40.920
to do real time event
[INAUDIBLE] integration stuff.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:42.110
Can you tell us more about that?

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:43.068
That's pretty exciting.

00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:46.110
VIKRUM NIJJAR: Yeah, so
it's an early preview.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:49.854
So you can find access to
it in our Google group.

00:16:49.854 --> 00:16:52.270
So if you go to the Firebase
Google Group, the public one,

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:54.380
and search for
Unity, you'll see one

00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:57.250
of our engineers
made a call out to be

00:16:57.250 --> 00:17:01.020
able to take a look at
one of the early examples.

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:05.260
We've had a steady drumbeat
of requests for Unity,

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:07.296
essentially since the beginning.

00:17:07.296 --> 00:17:08.890
MARK MANDEL: I'm not surprised.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:12.210
VIKRUM NIJJAR: And like
Sara mentioned earlier,

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:15.079
our first SDK was JavaScript.

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:18.089
The second and third ones
were iOS and Android.

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:22.926
And Unity for the
most part runs on C#.

00:17:22.926 --> 00:17:25.710
And the way that the
architecture there is we

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:29.450
didn't have a simple plugin
that we could have added

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:33.240
without a lot of heavy lifting.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:35.660
And so even though it
remained a priority,

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:39.380
we didn't have the bandwidth
to execute on that early on.

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:44.390
And it was once we joined Google
that we had the engineering

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:47.900
resources to be able to build
something to allow people

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:50.686
to use Firebase on Unity.

00:17:50.686 --> 00:17:52.900
MARK MANDEL: It's
still early days?

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:55.289
VIKRUM NIJJAR: So I
don't have any examples

00:17:55.289 --> 00:17:56.830
off the top of my
head of people that

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:58.120
have built games on it yet.

00:17:58.120 --> 00:17:59.826
MARK MANDEL: That
was my next question.

00:17:59.826 --> 00:18:02.140
VIKRUM NIJJAR: But soon.

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:05.920
Unity is huge across
all platforms.

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:10.800
And giving both indie developers
and big shops the ability

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:15.580
to add those sorts of
features to their games,

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:18.730
we realize is
extremely important.

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:21.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Now that
you mention indie developers,

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:22.580
there's something
that I really like

00:18:22.580 --> 00:18:26.790
about Firebase, which is it's
actually free for many cases.

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:27.700
What is the pricing?

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:29.570
I know that the free
tier is very nice.

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:31.730
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, it's
essentially free forever,

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:33.600
up to 100 concurrent
connections.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:35.790
So that means 100
simultaneous people

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:39.320
on your website or mobile
app at the same time, which

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:40.070
is actually a lot.

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:41.856
So it's a good
problem to have if you

00:18:41.856 --> 00:18:43.730
have 100 people on your
app at the same time.

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:45.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's very nice.

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:47.065
That basically takes
you already to the point

00:18:47.065 --> 00:18:49.407
where, well, maybe I should
start making money off this?

00:18:49.407 --> 00:18:50.240
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:52.210
VIKRUM NIJJAR: Yeah, if you
have 100 concurrent people,

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:53.250
and you're not making money--

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:54.958
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
don't think my webpage

00:18:54.958 --> 00:18:56.951
has [INAUDIBLE]
gotten 100 in a day.

00:18:56.951 --> 00:18:58.070
SARA ROBINSON: And
then on the free tier,

00:18:58.070 --> 00:18:59.736
you also get access
to Firebase hosting.

00:18:59.736 --> 00:19:03.010
So you can deploy it to your
own Firebase app.com domain.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:07.140
And then the self-service pay
plan start at $49 a month.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.396
And then the concurrent
limits go up from there.

00:19:10.396 --> 00:19:16.900
VIKRUM NIJJAR: There is also a
Spark plan, which is $5 a month

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:19.810
to give you the custom domain.

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:23.590
We've heard a lot of feedback
where $49 was too high

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:25.390
for the new developers.

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:27.360
Whereas the free
gives you some things,

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:31.960
the $5 plan gives you
your own white labeling,

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:33.710
and some of the features
of the heartland.

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:37.280
So we heard that feedback
from the indie devs,

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:40.070
so we have a $5 plan as well.

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:43.370
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:19:45.394 --> 00:19:46.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:46.310 --> 00:19:51.540
So you mentioned that
some time ago you

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:55.270
were together with
Parse in the same batch

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:57.200
for a YC, Y Combinator.

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:58.160
.

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:00.640
Later on, you got
acquired by Google.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:02.960
Parse got acquired by Facebook.

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:07.006
We all know Parse
ended up being shut

00:20:07.006 --> 00:20:08.130
down-- well, at some point.

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:10.360
And the migration
was great, but still,

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:14.430
some developers felt like
oh yeah, this was a problem.

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:17.150
What can you say to those that
might be a little bit concerned

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:18.525
about this happening
to Firebase?

00:20:18.525 --> 00:20:22.300
VIKRUM NIJJAR: So in
general, it was very sad,

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:24.820
the shutdown of Parse.

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:28.370
And we know that team.

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:31.690
It's just bad for the
ecosystem at large, let alone

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:38.100
for the developers and the
Parse team, which we know.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:39.650
And there are many
apps that were

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:42.280
built on Parse and Firebase,
and Firebase and Meteor.

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:44.920
And so they're not
completely siloed.

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.540
I think that the
thing to keep in mind

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:55.130
is what these sorts
of offerings provide,

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:58.450
whether it's Firebase
or Parse, is leverage

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:00.950
for developers to
build their products

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:03.010
and build their visions.

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:07.130
And as long as there
is a need for that,

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:11.080
I think this ecosystem will
continue to exist and flourish.

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:15.490
Whether the particular
circumstances around Parse

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:19.766
can be dissected, I think
it's pretty unambiguous

00:21:19.766 --> 00:21:27.670
that Google supports cloud
in general and developers,

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:28.285
in particular.

00:21:28.285 --> 00:21:29.660
SARA ROBINSON:
Yeah, I know there

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:31.460
is lots of exciting
Firebase features

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:33.300
that the team is working on.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:37.182
And Google's definitely
100% behind Firebase.

00:21:37.182 --> 00:21:38.390
VIKRUM NIJJAR: So stay tuned.

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:41.685
We have a lot to
announce at Google I/O.

00:21:41.685 --> 00:21:42.910
MARK MANDEL: Ooh.

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:44.600
I have to ask.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.280
You might tell me
no, but is there

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:49.450
anything you can maybe
hint at, or any new

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:53.330
features that we could
possibly get a little scoop on?

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:54.090
Just here?

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:56.381
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The only
stuff I know is confidential.

00:21:56.381 --> 00:21:57.630
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:58.850
What about you?

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:01.170
VIKRUM NIJJAR: The same.

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:06.010
I just say that I think it
will just reaffirm Google's

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:08.220
commitment to Firebase.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:11.790
Once you see what gets
announced and released at I/O,

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.310
I think there will
be no doubt as

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:18.420
to the commitment and longevity,
which is sort of envisioned

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:19.600
for Firebase.

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:20.804
SARA ROBINSON: Stay tuned.

00:22:20.804 --> 00:22:23.220
VIKRUM NIJJAR: I know that at
least in our team, Developer

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:26.420
[INAUDIBLE] for
Cloud, we are not

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:28.440
forced to talk about Firebase.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:32.260
It's something that is there,
but we choose to use it,

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:34.158
just because it's awesome.

00:22:34.158 --> 00:22:37.022
I don't think that's going to
be going away anytime soon.

